  "slug": "spatial-melanoma-a51a",
  "summary": "Spatially resolved transcriptomics across glioma, PDAC, HCC, HNSCC and other tumors now ties cell-state niches to outcome and therapy response.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is pairing spatial or single-cell transcriptomics with tissue architecture to place malignant, stromal and myeloid states within their niches. In glioblastoma, a CosMx study applies a Log-Gaussian Cox point-process framework to infer cell-type interactions from transcripts and pathway scores, while a separate study reports spatially restricted iron-metabolism stress programs that prime invasive mesenchymal states. Spatial work in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma defines an invasive myCAF subpopulation and spatial communities with TCGA survival associations, and clear cell renal cell carcinoma studies link spatial architecture to programmed cell death-associated immune niches. What is new is tying spatially resolved niches to invasion, immune context and prognosis, while the CloneTrast study infers T-cell clonal niches from paired scRNA and TCR-seq without spatial data.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-14"
}
